<p>U.S. prosecutors charged Mohammad Baqer Saad Dawood Al-Saadi, a 32-year-old Iraqi national whom authorities described as a senior member of the Iran-backed militia Kataib Hezbollah, with six terrorism-related counts after authorities brought him to New York and he appeared in federal court on Friday. The Justice Department accused him of directing or encouraging attacks against U.S. and Israeli interests, including alleged efforts to kill Americans and Jewish civilians in the United States and abroad. Prosecutors said Al-Saadi and his associates planned, coordinated or claimed responsibility for nearly 20 attacks and attempted attacks across Europe, Canada and the United States, including an alleged plan to bomb a New York synagogue and attacks targeting U.S. interests and Jewish sites. The complaint described the alleged campaign as retaliation connected to the war involving the United States, Israel and Iran, and identified the claimed attack network as Harakat Ashab al-Yamin al-Islamiya, which U.S. authorities said was tied to Kataib Hezbollah. Al-Saadi was ordered detained pending trial after appearing before a federal magistrate judge in Manhattan. U.S. officials said he was taken into American custody overseas and transported to the United States, while the Justice Department announcement did not specify where or when he was arrested.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/yIGSedEteXIl9L4C37GWnz4WGJlFM6fvJXGRI3-FaoukbGWK0Nxa9qt-RCCqV2t9OvXDoQHk00C6itQ46CtQt8SQ4B373FWHISBxQ1J17MlMiRxOevD5wAB2vNCTx7X-BAl46pWjwncDLQsehQHBSdjh-7c7LO4MlxtPpnAzwjIWilXXGmFVMyjNElGXyKqrKjRMVWgKUf4vQBf8h_9ByyUq0YlPr43HCU4W2VjRoYesTzacWWw65K1xs58flNQb27KpRl1xBiSrC89uTw' alt='Mohammad Al Saadi, accused of planning an attack on a synagogue, appears in federal court in Manhattan on Friday.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Named targets beyond New York: Allegations in the complaint cited plots or discussions involving Jewish institutions in Los Angeles and Scottsdale, as well as a synagogue and the U.S. consulate in Toronto.</li><li>European cases: Incidents cited in the complaint included an explosives attack on a Bank of New York Mellon branch in Amsterdam, an arson attack on a synagogue in North Macedonia and the stabbing of two Jewish people in London.</li><li>Online claims: Federal prosecutors said Al-Saadi posted Ashab al-Yamin-branded propaganda videos claiming responsibility for attacks, including recordings linked to incidents in Belgium, the Netherlands and the United Kingdom.</li><li>UK concern: The Guardian reported that the alleged wave had caused particular concern in the United Kingdom, where Jewish community centres, charities, synagogues and other sites had been targeted in recent weeks.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Manhattan U.S. Attorney Jay Clayton: Clayton said Al-Saadi “attempted to disrupt American society through intimidation and violence” in a statement announcing the case. (<a href='https://www.thenationalnews.com/news/us/2026/05/16/new-york-charges-iraqi-man-over-alleged-terror-plot-targeting-synagogue/'>The National</a>)</li><li>FBI Director Kash Patel: Patel described the transfer as a foreign custody transfer operation and thanked U.S. partners, including envoy Tom Barrack, for helping bring Al-Saadi to the United States. (<a href='https://www.foxnews.com/us/fbi-brings-iraqi-man-accused-coordinating-nearly-20-terror-attacks-europe-face-trial-new-york'>Fox News</a>)</li><li>Defense lawyer Andrew Dalack: Dalack told the court that Turkish authorities detained Al-Saadi in Istanbul and transferred him to U.S. custody without giving him a chance to challenge the case against him. (<a href='https://www.thenationalnews.com/news/us/2026/05/16/new-york-charges-iraqi-man-over-alleged-terror-plot-targeting-synagogue/'>The National</a>)</li><li>Turkish authorities: Turkey had not publicly commented on the reported arrest and transfer by the time Middle East Eye reported on the case. (<a href='https://www.middleeastey